ent ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{173}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{7}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059831 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41b43c0b-f0bb-54eb-9644-6db9398ab0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Sine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d049c6-6aba-53bf-a388-a1e3eb0ab7be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541443} {:answer/id #uuid "10e9538d-e22f-50b5-8ec6-bf62dd38f088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541458} {:answer/id #uuid "3b84d0df-0b34-535b-87eb-858a47ce9ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541550} {:answer/id #uuid "7e51a790-4d74-55a8-a528-02001d4ef24a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{320}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541680} {:answer/id #uuid "d014da4c-255a-5e0e-9770-25d48e9076fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{320}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09d049c6-6aba-53bf-a388-a1e3eb0ab7be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541443} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b5f97a-9764-5d9d-b0de-bd92fb59a8fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0cef338-42f6-5b2f-86eb-627321869565" :question/math-academy-id "q-1587" :question/problem "What is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{320}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{320}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${320}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/8d/8dbbe0dc938508097f1c43da788ff2a2fffb7e4346c5650179295a7de5a80d1f.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={320}^{∘}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={360}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{320}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{320}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{40}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{320}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{320}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{40}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1810cacd-3565-5438-ae93-45b50970f5dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Trigonometric Ratio Given a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7255de22-9755-582d-ab24-92838b834a2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541655} {:answer/id #uuid "a197e0aa-b6f0-550a-b993-6ba0800a00ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541758} {:answer/id #uuid "ac06ba10-5740-5a2b-b8b7-df08b5ce760b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541778} {:answer/id #uuid "d55eab2d-743f-5b14-96c8-4bdd90eaea9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541878} {:answer/id #uuid "e09ee1ef-3f56-5194-89ae-be7b1d0b6df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d55eab2d-743f-5b14-96c8-4bdd90eaea9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541878} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d536a18-c224-5697-bc60-1c4764f0376b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4900364d-1162-5845-8f68-07de4df04e97" :question/math-academy-id "q-64577" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a9381ee6a4c0fb541f9459dd8c31fd994228c9cf6b42a2c4579f72cab96c37ae.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-0.7,$ which of the following is correct?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fa/fab4d89dbe3697bb9e22b815939129bdb87f1919985edbf5c84628a1eb1d5853.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=-0.7$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-0.7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1810cacd-3565-5438-ae93-45b50970f5dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Trigonometric Ratio Given a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36a97c2b-3301-571e-82a7-831a259ccdd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541537} {:answer/id #uuid "6a365716-cf55-5a59-b47d-ebff77c54f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541635} {:answer/id #uuid "d02afbcd-6be1-58b5-881a-a2cb8e881877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541865} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d049d7-0b89-5023-a1a9-dcc2f8c17f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541910} {:answer/id #uuid "e8692296-cd80-50a1-95af-37d1ed311207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a365716-cf55-5a59-b47d-ebff77c54f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541635} :answer-field/id #uuid "c339f536-4fa7-5ebb-bd2a-cb831e6259f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7061407-4d39-5b42-8242-7b789dd439ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-64583" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a4b6e20d8ab249f0aab42f4c21cd9b8e2bf9c04fc34ba3462aa07175d797af9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-0.6,$ which of the following is correct?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b2/b269afd0b8fb3b82423a2f80b3dfb5aa39aeeea26d93d6d3220488456d310248.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=-0.6$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-0.6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "669295c7-5c63-5926-907c-1e2c211c3570" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Cosine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1baf1391-fef5-5ca5-81f7-b718e33d0414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541478} {:answer/id #uuid "4131b12a-0fcf-57ab-9612-46354b4fd9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541556} {:answer/id #uuid "817c85f0-9a1d-5b8c-8485-5cfad53e03c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{300}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541684} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9685b2-1cd2-5b88-a29e-364e31bf2620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541755} {:answer/id #uuid "d8502863-a146-578b-9ce5-4158af8863a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1baf1391-fef5-5ca5-81f7-b718e33d0414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541478} :answer-field/id #uuid "2db88f99-6d81-518b-b14b-2fe060ea0177" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f4f7b8b-fc7f-5b1e-bb19-2746ee23424b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64634" :question/problem "What is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{300}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{300}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${300}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/73/731a06693d32fde4db47283c1fd31e80891c9b4e05e4917b809379d442f410d0.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={300}^{∘}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={360}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{300}^{∘} \\\\  & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{300}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{300}^{∘}$ must be *positive* because the cosine ratio is *always* positive in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{300}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}.\n$$"}]}
